// test-error
// test-adr: 016
// Issue #1333: reopening COMPOSES a scope; it does not relax member uniqueness.
//
// This is the negative control for the reopening fix. An assertion catches
// under-enforcement (a scope that cannot be reopened); this catches
// OVER-enforcement -- a fix that stopped reporting genuine duplicate members
// because they happened to be written in different blocks.
//
// Members are grouped by the scope's own identity, not by the declaration block,
// so these two definitions of Lib.useIt collide exactly as they would inside one
// block.
//
// The assertion in .expected.error names the symbol, the code, and BOTH declaring
// lines. It could not before #1334: the conflict arrived through a second channel
// (`result.conflicts`) and the only thing reaching `result.errors` was a generic
// wrapper hardcoded at position 1:0, which named no symbol at all.
//
// The two line numbers are the point. They come from each member symbol's own
// position, so a scope reopened across files names the block each definition
// actually came from -- the original defect printed one location twice.

scope Lib {
    public u32 useIt() {
        return 1;
    }
}

scope Lib {
    public u32 useIt() {
        return 2;
    }
}

u32 main() {
    return Lib.useIt();
}
